Stride & Base =C2=B7 Weekly for patient runners

FOG, HEAT AND THE SUMMER YOU ACTUALLY HAVE

Half our readers are sweating through 90-degree mornings. The other half live=
 somewhere coastal and are wearing gloves in August. Both groups ask the same=
 question: does this weather ruin my training?

If it's hot: heat costs you roughly 1 to 3 percent of pace per 10=C2=B0F abov=
e 60=C2=B0F, more if it's humid. Run by effort, accept the slower splits, and=
 drink to thirst plus some sodium on runs over 75 minutes. Ten to fourteen da=
ys of regular heat exposure brings real adaptations (more plasma volume, earl=
ier sweating), which pay off on cooler race days.

If it's cool and foggy: lucky you, but don't skip hydration just because you =
don't feel hot. And if you're racing somewhere warmer this fall, two or three=
 sessions a week in extra layers or a warm gym will get you part of the heat =
adaptation.

If you just moved between the two: give yourself a week. New hills, new route=
s, new sleep. A drop in pace in week one is not lost fitness.

THIS WEEK'S SESSION
Hill strides: 8 x 20 seconds up a moderate grade at strong but relaxed effort=
, walk down. After an easy run, twice a week.

GEAR NOTE
Our long-term test of three daily trainers is out next week. Spoiler: the cus=
hioned one wore out at 310 miles.

